.TH OROC-LIST-DEVICES 1 "" "Oro Runtime 0.1.4" "Oro Runtime Manual"
.SH NAME
oroc\-list\-devices \- Get the list of connected devices.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
oroc list\-devices [options] \-\-platform=<platform>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Get the list of connected devices.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-platform=<platform>\fR
platform target to list devices for:
.br
\(bu android
.br
\(bu ios
.TP
\fB\-f, \-\-format=<format>\fR
print as 'text' or 'json'
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
shorthand for '\-\-format=json'
.TP
\fB\-\-ecid\fR
show device ECID (ios only)
.TP
\fB\-\-udid\fR
show device UDID (ios only)
.TP
\fB\-\-only\fR
only show ECID or UDID of the first device (ios only)
.TP
\fB\-V, \-\-verbose\fR
enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-file=<path>\fR
mirror logs to a JSON file
.SH NOTES
.PP
Android output mirrors the device identifiers returned by `adb devices`. iOS output can include the human\-readable simulator/device name plus ECID or UDID for scripting. JSON output emits an array of device objects with stable id fields for automation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
\fBoroc list\-devices \-\-platform=android\fR
list attached Android devices visible to adb
.TP
\fBoroc list\-devices \-\-platform=ios \-\-udid\fR
print iOS devices with UDID values you can pass to \-\-device
.TP
\fBoroc list\-devices \-\-platform=ios \-\-json\fR
print connected iOS devices as JSON for scripts or agents
.SH SEE ALSO
oroc(1)
